    what_it_does: 'The most abundant free amino acid in human plasma and skeletal muscle. Conditionally essential - endogenous synthesis is normally adequate but demand outstrips supply in trauma, burns, sepsis, critical illness, severe catabolic states. Primary respiratory fuel for enterocytes (small intestine extracts a large fraction of dietary and circulating glutamine on first pass per Windmueller & Spaeth 1974) - this is the mechanistic backbone of every gut claim made for it. Fuel for rapidly dividing immune cells. Precursor for glutathione (via glutamate), nucleotides, arginine (via citrulline), glucosamine, and the glutamate/GABA pool. Nitrogen shuttle and ammonia buffer - inter-organ nitrogen transport, which is the source of its main hepatic safety caveat. Tight-junction support - availability upregulates occludin and claudin expression. Best-evidenced endpoints: (1) post-infectious IBS-D with confirmed hyperpermeability at 15 g/day for 8 weeks (Zhou 2019 Gut, single unreplicated trial with striking effect); (2) IBS symptoms as low-FODMAP adjunct at 15 g/day for 6 weeks (Rastgoo 2021 Front Nutr, small supporting trial); (3) acute reduction in exercise-induced permeability markers pre-endurance-in-heat at 0.25-0.9 g/kg fat-free mass (Pugh 2017 dose-response). NULL for: general permeability in mixed clinical populations (Abbasi 2024 meta-analysis WMD 0.00 in IBD/HIV/ICU/burns populations), immune function in fed athletes (Gleeson 2008 corrective review), muscle protein synthesis or hypertrophy (Candow 2001 at 0.9 g/kg lean mass/day found no effect). CONTRAINDICATED in critical illness/multi-organ failure (REDOXS Heyland 2013 NEJM n=1,223 - 28-day mortality 32.4% vs 27.2% on glutamine arms).',
