    safety_notes: 'Adverse effect profile is generally mild and reversible (Daniele 2005 SR): nausea, headache, GI disturbance, menstrual disturbance, acne, pruritus, rash. EMA labels immune (severe allergic reactions with face swelling/dyspnoea/swallowing difficulty), skin (rash/urticaria/acne), nervous system (headache/dizziness), GI (nausea/abdominal pain) and reproductive (menstrual disorders) at unknown frequency. Documented rare events: ovarian hyperstimulation in unstimulated IVF cycle, isolated case report of drug-induced psychotic disorder. Preclinical safety is THIN - mutagenicity/carcinogenicity not tested; signs of liver toxicity in two repeat-dose rat studies (4-week and 26-week); adequate reproductive toxicity testing not done. AU regulatory: permitted in AUST-L listed medicines; freely OTC through pharmacies/health food stores/supermarkets. AUST-L does NOT mean efficacy-evaluated - sponsor self-certifies against permitted ingredients and indications; lower bar than EMA well-established-use marketing authorisation that underpins the 20 mg Ze 440 evidence base. **LIVE TGA SAFETY ADVISORY** on potential interaction with oral contraceptives (reports of unintended pregnancy on progestogen-only pill) - must be reproduced in any Body Recode material that mentions Chasteberry. AUST-L permitted indications are low-level ("supports premenstrual health," "helps relieve symptoms of PMS," "supports healthy menstrual cycle") - cannot claim to treat PMDD, infertility, prolactinoma or hyperprolactinaemia. Verify current TGA Permissible Indications Determination wording before publishing client-facing claims. Australian AUST-L product is not automatically equivalent to the Ze 440 extract that generated the evidence base - product selection is a live clinical decision.',
    coach_doctrine: 'Best-evidenced botanical for physical-symptom-predominant PMS - EMA well-established use grants marketing authorisation to a SPECIFIC extract (dry extract, DER 6-12:1, 60% m/m ethanol) at 20 mg once daily for PMS. ACOG (2023 Clinical Practice Guideline) and RCOG (Green-top 48) reviewed the same literature and explicitly declined to recommend, citing heterogeneity of formulations/doses and high risk of bias. Both positions defensible - operational reading is: **evidence supports a specific standardised product, NOT "chasteberry" as a generic category**. Client analogy: EU regulator licenses "amoxicillin 500 mg," ACOG asks about "an antibiotic." **20 mg is the dose CEILING** - Schellenberg 2012 dose-finding RCT (n=162 women, PMS, 3 cycles): 20 mg significantly better than placebo and 8 mg; 30 mg NOT superior to 20 mg. The commonly-quoted "20-40 mg" range in reference texts reflects DIFFERENT extracts with different drug-extract ratios (Ze 440 at 20 mg vs BNO 1095 at 40 mg), NOT a titration ladder within one product. Extract identity matters more than milligram count - agnuside is an analytical marker (delivered dose at licensed strength is under 0.1 mg/day) NOT a potency measure; the compounds with demonstrated D2 activity are diterpenes (rotundifuran, viteagnusin I) and triterpenes (3-epi-maslinic acid) which are NOT on the label. Product-selection checklist: (1) standardised dry extract with stated DER? (2) native extract weight declared (not just herb-equivalent)? (3) named clinical extract (Ze 440, BNO 1095)? (4) AUST-L number? (5) HASTA/Informed Sport batch-tested if athlete? Reject unquantified "1,000 mg equivalent dried fruit" products. **Dose-direction paradox** - underdosing can push prolactin the WRONG way: BP1095E1 study in healthy men showed 120 mg raised 24h prolactin secretion vs placebo while 480 mg lowered it; TRH testing showed +16% at 120 mg and -10% at 480 mg. Another argument for a defined standardised dose over "a bit of vitex." Continuous daily dosing through whole cycles (NOT luteal-phase only) - unlike SSRIs (which permit intermittent luteal dosing due to rapid onset), Chasteberry has no evidence base for intermittent use. Every positive trial dosed daily. Morning is conventional (dopaminergic-agent convention) but no head-to-head trial vs evening - consistency matters more than clock time. Minimum 3 cycles per EMA well-established use posology; nothing meaningful happens in cycle 1. Trial entry per Schellenberg: symptoms must increase >30% from follicular to luteal phase, verified prospectively. Two cycles of prospective symptom tracking (DRSP or equivalent) BEFORE starting per ACOG - otherwise you cannot tell whether it worked. Extension to 6 months is reasonable clinical practice but is BEYOND trial evidence and BEYOND EMA duration guidance; if a client is still symptomatic at 3 months, GP review is the evidence-aligned action, not a longer solo trial. **The most important AU-specific safety flag is the OCP interaction** - TGA advisory is LIVE (report of unplanned pregnancy on levonorgestrel POP). Must be reproduced in client-facing material. Especially POP/mini-pill. **Prolactinoma-masking is real** (documented case: Agnolyt masked a 2mm microadenoma for 3 months while cycles regularised) - amenorrhoea/galactorrhoea/oligomenorrhoea = prolactin + TSH + imaging via GP BEFORE any trial. **PMDD is a REFERRAL** not a supplement decision - affective symptoms define PMDD and Chasteberry is weakest exactly on affective endpoints; carries suicidality risk per ACOG. Perimenopausal use is coherent ONLY where cycles are still occurring and complaint is PMS/mastalgia-flavoured (shortening luteal phase, worsening premenstrual symptoms in a woman in her 40s) - it is NOT a menopause treatment; NOT for vasomotor symptoms (Black Cohosh is that tool). **REDs comes FIRST for athletes** - cycle irregularity in an under-fuelled athlete is functional-hypothalamic (suppressed GnRH pulsatility from energy deficit), NOT prolactin. Using Chasteberry to chase a regular cycle in an under-fuelled athlete is worse than useless - risks partially normalising bleeding pattern while bone/endocrine damage continues, and delays the intervention that actually works (restoring energy availability). Screen energy availability, training load, disordered eating FIRST; refer sports physician + APD. Also deliver alongside (higher guideline support than Chasteberry itself per ACOG): aerobic exercise / yoga / Pilates, calcium 1,000-1,200 mg/day (cross-references future Calcium entry), CBT if accessible. Coach language mirrors AUST-L permitted indications: "supports premenstrual health," "helps relieve symptoms of PMS," "supports healthy menstrual cycle" - NEVER "treats" PMDD/infertility/prolactinoma.',
