    category: 'womens_specific',
    short_description: 'A GAP-FILLING nutrient, NOT a default supplement. Different from every other library entry - requires answering "who is actually short?" before "does it work?" Australian intake is genuinely inadequate for the core BR cohort (90.7% of women aged 50-64 below the EAR from food per ABS 2023), but calcium supplementation in ALREADY-REPLETE community-dwelling adults shows no fracture benefit and measurable harms (kidney stones RR 1.11-1.20, contested CVD signal RR 1.15 in postmenopausal women per Myung 2021). Resolution: audit intake, close gap with food first, supplement only the shortfall, <=500 mg per dose, WITH MEALS (stone-risk mitigation), paired with vitamin D, separated from interacting medications. Coach cannot recommend calcium without articulating client current intake in mg.',
    what_it_does: 'Essential mineral; 99% stored in bone/teeth, 1% in blood/cells for muscle contraction/nerve signalling/clotting. NHMRC RDI (Australia): 1,000 mg for adults 19-70 (women), 19+ (men to 70); 1,300 mg for women 51+ (postmenopausal), men >70, adolescents 12-18, pregnancy/lactation 14-18. Upper Level 2,500 mg/day all adults. RDI covers TOTAL calcium from food + beverages + fortified products + supplements combined. Supplemental calcium fills a DIETARY GAP; it does not add to sufficiency. Evidence picture: (1) BONE MINERAL DENSITY reliably improves ~1% at hip with calcium+D (WHI n=36,282, 7 years). BMD is a surrogate - 1% density difference does not translate to meaningful fracture difference. (2) FRACTURE benefit is CONDITIONAL on baseline risk: institutionalised frail elderly (Chapuy Decalyos 1992) ~9 fewer hip fractures per 1,000/year with calcium+D; community-dwelling healthy adults ~1 fewer per 1,000/year (Cochrane 2014); USPSTF 2024 draft ZERO absolute difference per 1,000 (RR 0.99 for hip fracture, RR 1.11 for kidney stones). Same RR, radically different absolute benefit. (3) Vitamin D ALONE does NOT prevent hip fracture (RR 1.15 CI 0.99-1.33). (4) CARDIOVASCULAR signal contested but real - Bolland 2010/2011 identified ~25% RR increase for MI in supplement users; Chung 2016 (NOF-funded) found no association; Myung 2021 meta of 13 RCTs (n=28,935) found CVD RR 1.15 and CHD RR 1.16 in healthy postmenopausal women, ~8.6 more events per 1,000. Signal strongest where LARGE supplements added on top of ALREADY ADEQUATE diet. Dietary calcium is NOT implicated. (5) KIDNEY STONES: supplemental calcium associated with RR 1.11-1.20 for stones; dietary calcium is PROTECTIVE (Curhan cohorts: RR 0.73 for stones in highest vs lowest dietary calcium quintile) - paradox explained by gut oxalate binding when calcium taken WITH meals vs raised urinary calcium when taken fasted. (6) PERIMENOPAUSAL BONE WINDOW compressed not gradual: SWAN cohort shows BMD loss begins 1 year BEFORE final menstrual period, accelerates through ~3-year "transmenopause" window (~-2.46%/yr lumbar spine, -1.76%/yr femoral neck), decelerates but does not stop 2 years post-FMP. Cumulative 10-yr loss ~10.6% lumbar spine, 9.1% femoral neck. Highest-leverage bone period in a woman life - and LOADING THE SKELETON HARD (LIFTMOR ~4% lumbar spine BMD gain over 8 months of twice-weekly HiRIT, Watson/Beck Griffith University) produces a larger effect than any calcium trial.',
