    safety_notes: 'AU Upper Level = 2,500 mg/day for all adults including pregnancy/lactation. NHMRC set the UL conservatively at 2,500 mg from a lowest-observed-adverse-effect level of ~5 g (milk-alkali syndrome), applying an uncertainty factor of 2 to account for kidney stone frequency in AU/NZ and for interference with zinc and iron absorption. ABS 2023: fewer than 5% of Australians in any age/sex group exceed the calcium UL from food and beverages - meaning essentially all UL-exceeding intake comes from supplements. Absorption ceiling ~500 mg elemental per dose (NHMRC); above this only 5-10% of the excess is absorbed. Kidney stone signal well-established for SUPPLEMENTAL calcium (WHI CaD: HR 1.17 CI 1.02-1.34, ~2 more stone formers per 1,000; USPSTF 2024 draft RR 1.11 CI 1.03-1.21). Cardiovascular signal contested but non-trivial: Myung 2021 meta of 13 RCTs (n=28,935) found CVD RR 1.15 (CI 1.06-1.25) and CHD RR 1.16 (CI 1.05-1.28) in postmenopausal women, ~8.6 more CVD events per 1,000. Sensitivity: excluding Bolland 2011 WHI reanalysis attenuates the signal. Chung 2016 (NOF-funded via Pfizer) found no association. Dietary calcium NOT implicated. Take WITH MEALS is a STONE-RISK instruction not just an absorption instruction - gut oxalate binding requires food. Milk-alkali syndrome: rare at supplement doses in normal renal/parathyroid function but classical toxicity endpoint - fatigue, constipation, polyuria/thirst, muscle weakness, confusion, new kidney stones. AU regulatory: unscheduled OTC (AUST-L listed or food-standard); labels declare ELEMENTAL calcium (always read that figure, not salt weight - "1,500 mg calcium carbonate" = ~600 mg elemental; "1,000 mg calcium citrate" = ~210 mg elemental). Products vary widely in form and elemental content. Calcium is one of few ingredients with an APPROVED restricted representation - TGA permits osteoporosis-related advertising for products delivering >=290 mg elemental per daily dose (25% of RDI baseline), accompanied by wording that it is effective where diet is high in calcium with adequate vitamin D status. NRVs under NHMRC rolling review with updated values anticipated during 2026 - re-check nrv.gov.au and eatforhealth.gov.au before next review cycle. USPSTF 2024 recommendation was still in DRAFT as of July 2026 - check for published statement.',
    coach_doctrine: 'GAP-FILLING NUTRIENT, NOT a default supplement. THIS IS UNIQUE IN THE LIBRARY. Every other entry answers "does it work?" - this one requires also answering "who is actually short?" **"If you cannot articulate the client current daily calcium intake in mg, you are not in a position to recommend a calcium supplement."** Single most important sentence in the monograph. Australian intake is genuinely inadequate for the core BR cohort (90.7% of women aged 50-64 below EAR from food per ABS 2023; 93.2% of Australian females 12-17; 87.6% of women 65-74) - the audit is not a formality, the gap is usually there. But: supplementation in ALREADY-REPLETE community-dwelling adults shows NO fracture benefit and measurable harms (WHI kidney stones HR 1.17; USPSTF 2024 draft hip fracture RR 0.99 = zero per 1,000; Myung 2021 CVD RR 1.15 in postmenopausal women, ~8.6 more CVD events per 1,000). Resolution: audit intake, close gap with FOOD FIRST (one extra dairy or fortified serve = ~300 mg, cheaper, better tolerated, no stone/CVD signal), supplement ONLY the shortfall. Supplemental dose is the SHORTFALL, NOT the RDI. Worked example: 54yo woman with flat white + yoghurt + background ~780 mg vs RDI 1,300 mg = shortfall 520 mg = ONE 500 mg dose. NOT a 1,200 mg product (which pushes her total to ~1,980 mg and into the WHI harm-signal range). 500 mg ELEMENTAL per dose is the absorption ceiling (NHMRC) - above 500 mg only 5-10% of excess is absorbed AND contributes to constipation and raises serum calcium acutely (the mechanism proposed for CVD signal). Split into 2 doses if shortfall exceeds 500 mg. **NON-NEGOTIABLE.** WITH MEALS is a STONE-RISK instruction, not primarily absorption - calcium eaten with a meal binds gut oxalate and reduces urinary calcium; taken fasted it raises urinary calcium without the compensating oxalate reduction. Applies to every form including citrate and bisglycinate (which do not need food for absorption). Form choice = TOLERABILITY not proven fracture advantage. Citrate or bisglycinate as defaults because carbonate is fragile (constipation dominant reason clients quit; carbonate absorption collapses in PPI users unless strictly with meals). Bisglycinate has NO outcome trials - sold on tolerability, evidence thin. Carbonate is not wrong; it is fragile. Read ELEMENTAL calcium on the label, not salt weight ("1,500 mg calcium carbonate" = ~600 mg elemental). Fracture evidence is CONDITIONAL on baseline risk - Cochrane 2014: same RR 0.84 for hip fracture, but ~9 fewer per 1,000/year in institutionalised frail (Chapuy Decalyos population) vs ~1 fewer per 1,000/year in community-dwelling. Do NOT sell to a healthy 55yo BR client using the institutional effect size. Vitamin D + calcium is the evidence base, NOT calcium alone - vitamin D alone does NOT prevent hip fracture (RR 1.15). Calcium alone is NOT sufficient for established osteoporosis - pharmacotherapy is the intervention (MHT, bisphosphonates, denosumab) - REFER. **The largest lever in the perimenopausal bone window is LOADING THE SKELETON HARD, not calcium.** LIFTMOR (Watson/Beck, Griffith University AU 2018) - twice-weekly HiRIT produced ~4% lumbar spine BMD gain over 8 months, LARGER than anything the calcium literature offers, directly within S&ES scope. This IS the headline intervention; calcium is the substrate. SWAN cohort: perimenopausal bone loss is COMPRESSED not gradual (begins 1 year before FMP, ~-2.46%/yr lumbar spine and -1.76%/yr femoral neck through 3-year transmenopause window, decelerates but does not stop 2 years post-FMP; cumulative 10-yr loss 10.6% lumbar spine and 9.1% femoral neck). Highest-leverage bone period in a woman life - biggest coach add-value window - most supplement marketing target. Adequate protein supports bone; low protein intake is a risk factor. Do NOT use "protein leaches calcium from bone" as a reason to reduce protein in older clients - net effect is favourable. Levothyroxine 4-hour separation is the most clinically consequential calcium interaction in general practice - carbonate/citrate/acetate ALL reduce absorption; not just a carbonate issue. If client has been co-administering, flag to prescriber - correcting timing may effectively increase their thyroxine dose and warrant retesting. Iron and zinc 2-hour separation. Bisphosphonates per prescriber (typically fasted first thing with water, upright, nothing else 30-60 min; calcium much later). Coach language mirrors AUST-L permitted low-level indications. Calcium is one of few ingredients with approved restricted representation for osteoporosis advertising (>=290 mg elemental daily dose = 25% RDI). Cross-references: Vitamin D3+K2 entry (essential pairing - K2 proposed to direct calcium to bone), Iron entry (2h separation), Zinc entry (2h separation), Magnesium entry (frequently co-formulated, relevant to bone and GI tolerance), Chasteberry entry (ACOG recommends 1,000-1,200 mg calcium alongside for PMS as higher guideline-support than Chasteberry itself).',
