    safety_notes: 'Well tolerated at typical doses. GI upset (bloating, loose stools) is the common complaint, usually transient and dose-related. Allergy is real: marine contraindicated in fish/shellfish allergy, bovine/porcine sources in matched allergies. Kidney: hydroxyproline metabolises to oxalate - CKD or calcium-oxalate stone history is a hard flag requiring GP/nephrologist referral before use. Long-term data beyond ~12 months is sparse across the board. Vitamin C: keep total intake below 2,000 mg/day UL and note the adaptation-blunting caveat - chronic high-dose antioxidant supplementation may blunt physiological adaptation per AIS (same intervention we caution against elsewhere in the library). Dietary vitamin C adequacy first (kiwifruit, citrus, capsicum); use supplemental only in the pre-loading dose during defined rehab blocks. AU regulatory: TGA allows collagen products either as foods or listed complementary medicines (AUST-L). AUST-L requires low-risk ingredients and permits only low-level indications - a compliant label CANNOT claim to treat tendinopathy, OA or osteoporosis. If a product does, that is a compliance problem not evidence of efficacy. Peptide specification and dose vary widely between labels - read the amino acid or peptide spec, not the front panel. AIS Sports Supplement Framework: Group B (emerging scientific support requiring further research). AIS position: collagen is an adjunct within an injury-prevention or management protocol, not a replacement for well-established processes; research still in infancy on optimal doses, formulations and contraindications. Anti-doping for tested athletes: Sport Integrity Australia 2022 survey of 200 AU online sports supplements found 35% contained WADA-prohibited substances. Use only HASTA or Informed Sport batch-tested products verified by batch number in Sport Integrity App. **Gelatin as a food AVOIDS the batch-testing problem entirely** - a genuinely underused option for budget-constrained or high-risk athletes, especially made into jellies or jubes.',
    coach_doctrine: 'Connective tissue ADAPTATION slot, NOT a protein entry. The Whey Protein entry doctrine survives every test the last three years have thrown at it - Aussieker 2023 MSSE: 30g whey vs 30g collagen vs placebo post-resistance, collagen did NOT increase myofibrillar or muscle connective protein synthesis. Do NOT count collagen grams toward a client daily protein target. **The load does the work.** Musculoskeletal collagen is non-responsive to feeding in the absence of exercise - exercise is required to switch on the synthetic machinery in muscle and connective tissue. A client doing rehab properly WITHOUT collagen will out-adapt a client taking collagen WITHOUT rehab, every time. No load, no point. This is the single most important coaching point in the entry. **Dose has moved from the 15g convention to 20-30g** per Nulty 2024 dose-response and AIS position. The 15g in the original Shaw 2017 research reflected blinding constraints, not a ceiling. AIS recommends minimum 20g for optimal amino acid availability around exercise; some evidence for 30g dose-dependent PINP AUC advantage. **Vitamin C is obligate cofactor** for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase - established biochemistry - BUT the 500mg dose is trial convention not established requirement, AND chronic high-dose antioxidant supplementation may BLUNT physiological adaptation (same intervention we caution against elsewhere). **Practical resolution: dietary vitamin C adequacy FIRST (kiwifruit, citrus, capsicum); use supplemental 50-500mg with the pre-loading dose ONLY during defined rehab or adaptation blocks - NOT as a standing daily dose.** Frequency matched to LOADING SESSIONS not to days. Timing 30-60 min pre-loading is the AIS-recommended window (40-60 min post-ingestion peak amino acid availability). Note the timing wrinkle: Lee 2023 dosed post-session and got positive tendon stiffness; Lee 2024 dosed pre-session and also got positive. Pre-loading rationale is mechanistically sound but pre-vs-post has NOT been cleanly demonstrated superior for chronic tendon outcomes. Default to pre-loading; do not oversell it. Source does not matter clinically - AIS: no significant difference in plasma AA availability between bovine, porcine, marine. Marine premium is cosmetic marketing. Bone broth is too variable to serve as therapeutic source. Vegan collagen has insufficient evidence for equivalence. **Gelatin as food avoids the batch-testing problem entirely** for tested athletes - underused option (jellies, jubes). GRADE certainty is LOW to VERY LOW for the tendon outcomes we most care about (Bischof 2024). Buell 2026 flags critical confound: dose, form, and vitamin C are entirely confounded in the aggregate literature (positive Lee/Nulty at 30g WITH vit C; negative Jerger at 5g WITHOUT vit C - cannot attribute superior outcomes to dose alone). Women are badly under-represented in the trial base (Buell 2026: 246 male, 11 female across 8 RCTs). Loading structure supports SHORT SPACED BOUTS rather than long sessions - 10 min activity + 6h rest > continuous activity per engineered ligament work. In rehab: 2-3 brief isometric or heavy-slow sessions per day, 6h apart, with collagen before at least two of them - under physiotherapy direction. What marketing OVERSELLS: muscle building/hypertrophy (unsupported - Whey doctrine intact), recovery from training (weak, some 48h reactive-strength signal at low certainty), gut healing/leaky gut (NOT supported - human evidence is two small industry-funded studies, no meta-analyses; glutamine mechanism is separate, see L-Glutamine entry), anti-ageing/longevity (skin signal is real and modest, longevity claims are invented), marine > bovine (no clinical superiority), multi-collagen blends (marketing construct), hair and nails (weakest of cosmetic endpoints). Bone: Konig 2018 (131 postmenopausal women, 12 months, 5g/day FORTIBONE) real signal but single research group, GELITA-funded, no independent replication yet. Cross-reference Calcium + D3+K2 entries for the postmenopausal bone protocol. Osteoarthritis: **NOT ESCEO-endorsed** (2014 and 2019 ESCEO algorithms recommend crystalline glucosamine sulfate + chondroitin sulfate as SYSADOAs, not collagen). UC-II 40mg is a DIFFERENT product with a DIFFERENT mechanism (oral tolerance immune-mediated) - do NOT substitute for hydrolysed collagen. AUST-L labels cannot claim to treat tendinopathy, OA or osteoporosis - low-level indications only. Coach role for tendinopathy is coordination with physio/sports-med, not solo prescription. Endpoint-matched branded peptides (TENDOFORTE, FORTIBONE, VERISOL) justify the premium price ONLY where the client endpoint matches the trial endpoint - do NOT pay TENDOFORTE prices for general skin support. Cross-references: Whey Protein (separate slot, DIAAS 0), Glycine (rate-limiting substrate + Melendez-Hevia deficit; Glycine Elite tier covers substrate loading), L-Glutamine (actual gut barrier mechanism collagen marketing borrows), Calcium + D3+K2 (bone matrix + mineralisation context), Vitamin C (cofactor + adaptation-blunting caveat).',
