    safety_notes: 'Tolerability is genuinely good - eight fasting healthy males at 2/5/10/15 g single doses had NO side effects; only citrulline/ornithine/arginine plasma concentrations affected; urinary citrulline excretion <5%; plasma insulin and growth hormone unaffected. Well tolerated at 15 g. Across exercise literature: mild GI discomfort in ~15% at 8 g (Perez-Guisado 2010) - most likely reason clients quit. Warn hypertrophy clients. NO liver, renal or cardiovascular safety signals. **Interaction corrections from my brief:** organic nitrates (angina - nitroglycerin, isosorbide) are the MAJOR interaction (do NOT combine, refer prescriber immediately); PDE5 inhibitors are MONITOR-level (not contraindication), additive vasodilation plausible - refer prescriber; antihypertensives are moderate (refer prescriber); existing hypotension or orthostatic symptoms - do not initiate. HSV concern is theoretical only (extrapolated from arginine literature, no citrulline-specific human data) - do not invent a contraindication. AU regulatory: NOT WADA-prohibited (2026 List). AIS Framework: **NOT LISTED in Groups A or B** (my brief was wrong). Defaults to Group C - "not advocated for use by athletes within supplement programs." For institute athletes any use requires specific approval from Sports Institute/Academy or NSO Sports Supplement Panel. TGA (30 Nov 2023 declaration): bulk citrulline malate powder with performance claim is generally OUTSIDE the declaration and sits in food framework; capsules/tablets with performance claims are therapeutic goods requiring AUST-L listing + GMP + Advertising Code compliance. If a capsule product makes sport claims without AUST-L number that is a compliance red flag about the sponsor. Sport Integrity Australia HASTA survey (Barker 2025): 35% of 200 AU online sports supplements contained WADA-prohibited substances. HASTA or Informed Sport batch-tested product mandatory for tested athletes, batch verified in Sport Integrity App, screenshots retained. **Mahboobi 2019 BP meta (-7.54/-3.77 mmHg) is RETRACTED** - do not cite; still circulating on supplement blogs. Cormio 2011 ED trial is SINGLE-blind (not double-blind), sequential placebo-then-citrulline (placebo month always first so period and expectancy effects uncontrolled), n=24, never replicated - marketing outruns this data hard. Any client raising ED goes to GP - ED is a vascular and metabolic sentinel, we do not counsel around it.',
    coach_doctrine: 'MARGINAL AID with LOW-CERTAINTY evidence, framed explicitly as n-of-1 trial NOT prescription. Wang 2026 three-level meta (30 RCTs, 138 effect sizes, n=644): overall exercise performance g=0.16 (0.05-0.27) p=0.01, moderate heterogeneity I^2=45%, LOW statistical power, prediction interval -0.37 to 0.69, GRADE low to very low. Acute single-dose g=0.16; chronic did NOT reach significance. Aerobic endurance g=0.32 and anaerobic g=0.28 significant; **muscular endurance g=0.15 p=0.09 and strength/power g=0.10 p=0.28 NOT significant.** Effect COLLAPSES when Perez-Guisado 2010 removed (g=0.10 p=0.06). No dose-response (beta=-0.003 p=0.94). **AIS Framework CORRECTION:** citrulline is NOT AIS Group B (my brief was wrong). Unlisted in Groups A/B; defaults to Group C ("not advocated for use by athletes within supplement programs"). AIS has moved BCAA/HMB/phosphate/tyrosine/vitamin E from B to C - direction of travel for a GRADE-low g=0.16 substance is not upward. **Perez-Guisado 2010 corrections:** 41 men crossover with 8 g CM vs placebo, 8 sets bench at 80% 1RM to fatigue - significant from set 3, 18% more reps at S4, 53% at final set (NOT "14% across 8 sets" as my brief stated), 40% less soreness at 24/48h, 15% GI discomfort. Effect concentrated in fatigued back-half of session - mechanistically coherent, honest thing to tell hypertrophy clients. **Trexler 2019 is Sports Medicine NOT JISSN** (attribution corrected) - 12 studies n=198 SMD 0.20 (0.01-0.39). Confidence interval already borderline in 2019; Wang 2026 delivered the predicted collapse. **Malate probably contributes NOTHING ergogenic** per Wang 2026 - rationale is pharmaceutical (stable salt complex + neutralize inherent alkalinity of citrulline for palatability + gastric tolerance) NOT ergogenic. Head-to-head trials show no CM advantage over L-citrulline at matched dose. Do NOT tell clients malate adds an ergogenic layer. **CM ratio problem:** commercial "2:1" products often assay 1.11:1 to 1.92:1. 8 g CM at assayed 1.11:1 delivers ~4.2 g citrulline (not labelled ~5 g). Teach clients to look for products declaring the ratio - stop quoting single L-citrulline equivalent for CM. **60 min pre-session (NOT 40-60 min).** The 40-45 min window did NOT show effect in 2026 moderator analysis; PK peak plasma at ~1h. Amend protocol. **8 g is convention NOT dose-response evidence** - Wang 2026 subgroup contrasts not significant, meta-regression flat. Do not chase higher doses. **Powder-in-fluid is evidence-aligned format** (26 of 30 acute studies used beverages; capsule crossover trial at 4.4 g failed). Also keeps product outside TGA declaration. **BP is BETTER-supported indication than resistance training** - meta of 14 trials L-citrulline reduced brachial SBP -4.49 mmHg / DBP -3.63 mmHg; middle-aged/elderly meta of 15 RCTs (n=415) SBP -4.02 / DBP -2.54 mmHg. Market presents this backwards. GP-supervised lifestyle adjunct only. 3-6 g L-citrulline daily chronic. **Mahboobi 2019 BP meta is RETRACTED** - do NOT cite. **Cormio 2011 ED trial is single-blind (NOT double-blind), sequential, n=24, never replicated.** Marketing outruns this data hard. ED clients go to GP. **Organic nitrates are the MAJOR interaction** (not PDE5i as my brief said). PDE5i is monitor-level. Antihypertensives moderate. **NaHCO3 / nitrate stacking:** Cutrufello 2015 best-designed test found NO additive benefit for citrulline + beetroot. Do NOT routinely combine. With arginine = pointless (citrulline IS the arginine delivery vehicle). With creatine + beta-alanine: independent mechanisms, no interaction evidence, no synergy expectation (Trexler 2019 excluded co-supplementation studies so isolated evidence base tells us nothing). **Female evidence is UNMEASURED not smaller** (only 3 of 30 trials female-only in Wang 2026, cycle-phase control varied). Say honestly. Do NOT imply it does not work in women. **Trained-lifter tool NOT beginner tool** (Wang 2026: trained g=0.22 significant, untrained g=0.03 not). Population-level effect is small and fragile - **n-of-1 trial is the only honest way to know if a given client is a responder.** 4-6 sessions of structured tracking (reps to failure or session tonnage) against matched training with defined stop rule, or do not bother. Reasonable candidates: (1) high-volume hypertrophy-focused resistance trainers running multiple sets to or near failure where back-half-of-session effect has somewhere to express; (2) older adults with elevated BP as GP-supervised lifestyle adjunct. NOT indicated: pure endurance, strength/1RM specialists (creatine wins), body-composition-driven clients, general perimenopausal cohort as default, beginners, anyone unwilling to commit to structured n-of-1 trial. Commercial narrative (pumps, NO boosting, endurance, erections, wellness) runs far ahead of the evidence. BR differentiation is saying so. Support-tier framing: coach identifies + educates + supports n-of-1 trial + monitors; AccSD is appropriate prescriber for anything beyond marginal-aid trial; GP handles medication interaction screening. Cross-references: Beta-Alanine (independent mechanism, no interaction, no synergy expectation), Sodium Bicarbonate (independent mechanism, do NOT stack for synergy claim), Creatine (higher-yield first choice for strength/power), Beetroot (do NOT combine per Cutrufello 2015 - redundant).',
